<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/advanced/speciation-and-phylogenetics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Speciation is evolution's branching moment: <strong>one lineage becoming two</strong>. It
    requires a split (geographic or behavioural), enough divergence, and finally reproductive
    isolation that makes the split permanent. Phylogenetics then reconstructs the resulting
    tree — who split from whom, and roughly when — from DNA, morphology and fossils together.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-step speciation sequence — and the ring species caught in the act" widget="equationStep" config={specCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the tree of life professionally</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Allopatric vs sympatric:</strong> most speciation needs geography (allopatric); some happens within one range — apple maggot flies split from hawthorn flies by switching host fruit, in the same orchards.</li>
    <li><strong>Molecular clocks date the splits:</strong> genetic distance accumulates roughly steadily, calibrating divergence times against fossils — human–chimp split ~7 million years, mostly.</li>
    <li><strong>Hybridisation blurs branches:</strong> grizzly–polar bear hybrids exist; our own ancestors mixed with Neanderthals. Trees are real but "webbed" at the edges — especially in plants.</li>
    <li><strong>Phylogenetics is practical:</strong> it tracks viral lineages (COVID variants are a living phylogeny), pinpoints illegal timber and ivory origins, and guides drug discovery (looking for relatives of known medicinal species).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mayr, Systematics and the Origin of Species; Grant & Grant (finches); Futuyma, Evolution.</p>
</TopicPage>
